@page "/privacy"
@using Onyx.Data.ApiSchema
@layout LandingPageLayout
@inject IServerSettings ServerSettings

<PageTitle>Privacy policy · Onyx</PageTitle>

@*
    REVIEW BEFORE PUBLISHING: this describes what the code collects as of this version. The operator and contact
    come from the server settings (Onyx__Operator, Onyx__ContactEmail); the legal basis and retention period
    should be checked by someone qualified (GDPR, Google Play and Microsoft Store requirements).
*@

<div class="landing-page-body">
<MudContainer MaxWidth="MaxWidth.Medium" Class="py-6 privacy-policy">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h4" Class="mb-1">Privacy policy</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-4" Style="color: var(--mud-palette-text-secondary)">
        For the Onyx server at @ServerSettings.ServerUrl
    </MudText>

    <MudPaper Class="pa-5" Elevation="1">

    <MudText Typo="Typo.h6" Class="mb-1 privacy-heading">What Onyx collects</MudText>
    <MudText Class="mb-2">The Onyx apps record which apps you use and for how long, so you can see it on your dashboard. For each device they upload:</MudText>
    <ul class="mb-3" style="padding-left: 1.5rem">
        <li>the name and platform of the device (for example "Pixel 9", Android), chosen when it registers;</li>
        <li>for every hour: which apps were in the foreground and for how many minutes;</li>
        <li>for each app: its identifier (package or program name), display name, category reported by the system, and icon;</li>
        <li>only if you turn on website recording on a computer: for every hour, which websites (domain only, such
            as "github.com") were open in the active browser tab and for how many minutes.</li>
    </ul>
    <MudText Class="mb-2">
        Onyx does <b>not</b> record what you do inside apps: no window titles, full web addresses, page contents,
        messages, keystrokes, screenshots or location. Time while the device is locked or idle for five minutes is not counted on
        computers; on Android, Onyx reads the usage history the system keeps.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Class="mb-2">
        For your account Onyx stores your user name, email address and a hash of your password (never the password
        itself), and, if you use them, phone number and two-factor settings.
    </MudText>

    <MudText Typo="Typo.h6" Class="mt-5 mb-1 privacy-heading">Websites</MudText>
    <MudText Class="mb-2">
        Website recording is off by default and turned on separately on each computer. It needs the Onyx Websites
        browser extension, which reads the address of the active tab, reduces it to the domain, and passes the
        domain only to the Onyx app on the same computer (never directly to the internet). Private windows are never
        recorded. The list of websites you visit can reveal sensitive information, such as health, religious or
        political views; you can exclude websites, delete the website history at any time in the settings, and
        turn recording off again. Your excluded websites are stored with your account so every device respects them.
    </MudText>

    <MudText Typo="Typo.h6" Class="mt-5 mb-1 privacy-heading">Permissions</MudText>
    <MudText Class="mb-2">
        On Android, Onyx needs "usage access", which you grant in the system settings, to read which apps were used.
        It reads the list of installed launcher apps to show their names and icons.
    </MudText>

    <MudText Typo="Typo.h6" Class="mt-5 mb-1 privacy-heading">Who can see it</MudText>
    <MudText Class="mb-2">
        Your usage data is visible to you when signed in, and stored on the Onyx server operated by
        <b>@(m_Info?.Operator ?? "the person or organisation running this server")</b>. It is not sold or shared with third parties. App icons are
        stored once per app and shared between accounts; they contain no personal data.
    </MudText>

    <MudText Typo="Typo.h6" Class="mt-5 mb-1 privacy-heading">Deleting your data</MudText>
    <MudText Class="mb-2">
        Removing a device on the Devices page deletes all usage it uploaded. Deleting your account in the profile
        settings deletes the account, all devices and all usage data. "Delete website history" in the settings removes
        the websites from all usage data while keeping the browser time. Uninstalling the app stops collection; data
        already uploaded stays until you delete it.
    </MudText>

    <MudText Typo="Typo.h6" Class="mt-5 mb-1 privacy-heading">Contact</MudText>
    <MudText>
        Questions and requests about your data:
        @if (m_Info?.ContactEmail is { } email)
        {
            <MudLink Href="@($"mailto:{email}")">@email</MudLink>
        }
        else
        {
            <span>the operator of this server.</span>
        }
    </MudText>
    </MudPaper>
</MudContainer>
</div>

@code {
    private ServerInfoDto? m_Info;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync() => m_Info = await ServerSettings.GetInfoAsync();
}
